# Secrets — critical. The Dockerfile does `COPY . /app`, so without this
# line the .env file (containing GEMINI_API_KEY) is baked into an image
# layer and recoverable by anyone who pulls the image, even if it's later
# deleted in a subsequent layer. docker-compose passes the key in at run
# time via `env_file`, which is the correct mechanism.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Version control
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# Python caches and build artifacts
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
build/
dist/

# Virtual environments — never copy a host venv into a Linux image
.venv/
venv/

# Editor / OS cruft
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store

# Not needed at runtime; excluded to keep the image small and the build fast.
#
# Listed file by file rather than as `docs/`: the web app loads its logo
# from docs/assets/logo.jpg for the page icon, sidebar and chat avatar, and
# excluding the whole directory meant the image could never render the
# page - Streamlit raised MediaFileStorageError on the first load. The
# health check did not notice, because /_stcore/health reports the server,
# not the page. tests/test_config.py now fails if any file the app loads
# at runtime is excluded here.
docs/*.md
docs/training/
eval/report.md
benchmark_results.json

# Training-only artifacts — verified unused at runtime, ~72 MB of the image.
# The app loads models/language_classifier.tflite and tokenizer_config.json.
# It never loads these two: no caller passes `fallback_keras_path` to
# get_language_detector(), and tokenizer.pickle is referenced nowhere in
# the codebase at all. The .keras file is input to convert_model.py, which
# is a one-off conversion step, not part of serving.
# They stay in the repo (tracked via Git LFS) — they just don't belong in
# a deployment image.
ultimate_african_ai.keras
tokenizer.pickle
